===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|ceramics}} The process of applying [[heat]] or [[fire]], especially to [[clay]], etc., to produce [[pottery]].
#: {{ux|en|After the pots have been glazed, they go back into the kiln for a second '''firing'''.}}
# The [[fuel]] for a fire.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Tempest|2|2|text=No more dams I’ll make for fish;<br>Nor fetch in '''firing'''<br>At requiring {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Orwell Down and Out|chapter=25|passage=Downstairs there was a kitchen common to all lodgers, with free '''firing''' and a supply of cooking-pots, tea-basins, and toasting-forks.}}
# The act of adding fuel to a fire.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1945|month=July and August|title=Notes and News: "A Nice Day's Work"|journal=Railway Magazine|page=235|text=One driver told him that a fireman who did not know the route might easily go "over the side" between Corby and Harringworth, unless warned beforehand of the reverse curves; he himself, in his '''firing''' days, used to try so to plan his '''firing''' that it was unnecessary to add any more coal while passing over this stretch at speed.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1961|month=February|author='Balmore'|title=Driving and firing modern French steam locomotives - Part One|journal=Trains Illustrated|page=109|text=The doors are at the right level for '''firing''', which normally is down one side of the firebox at a time, unlike our own practice, which is to fire each side of the firebox with alternate shovelfuls.}}
# The [[discharge]] of a [[gun]] or other [[weapon]].
#* {{RQ:Defoe Robinson Crusoe|page=308|text={{...}} they fir’d several Times, making other Signals for the Boat. At last, when all their Signals and '''Firings''' prov’d fruitless, and they found the Boat did not stir, we saw them by the Help of my Glasses, hoist another Boat out, and row towards the Shore {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1940|author=w:Ernest Hemingway|title=w:For Whom the Bell Tolls|location=London|publisher=Jonathan Cape|chapter=43|page=417|url=https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.463378
|passage=He heard the '''firing''' and as he walked he felt it in the pit of his stomach as though it echoed on his own diaphragm.}}
# The [[dismissal]] of someone from a [[job]] due to poor work performance.
#* {{quote-journal|en|author={{w|Matthew d'Ancona|Matthew d’Ancona}}|title=Theresa May’s Shock Therapy|journal=w:The New York Times|date=19 July 2016|titleurl=https://www.nytimes.com/2016/07/20/opinion/theresa-mays-shock-therapy.html
|passage=Even the most seasoned analysts of British politics were struck by the brutality of Ms. May’s hirings and '''firings'''.}}
# [[cauterization|Cauterization]].

